---

<Page title="Pre-seed: the round and the risks" description={"Pre-seed, $750K on a post-money SAFE at a $7.5M cap, 18 months, five milestones with pass lines written first. Neural networks that report what broke while they train, and fix it."} ogImagePath="/og/invest-v3.png" trail={trail}>
	<PageBanner
		eyebrow="INVEST"
		title="The system of record for what training did."
		trail={trail}
	/>

	<section class="section-tight container reveal">
		<div class="iv-brief">
			<p class="iv-brief-label">The whole thing, in one screen</p>
			<dl class="iv-brief-list">
				{brief.map((b) => (
					<div class="iv-brief-row">
						<dt>{b.k}</dt>
						<dd>{b.v}</dd>
					</div>
				))}
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container">
		<div class="iv-thesis">
			<p class="iv-thesis-lead">
				Every model should arrive with a record of what its training did, part by part. Today none
				does.
			</p>
			<div class="iv-thesis-body">
				<p class="iv-thesis-p">
					When a training run breaks, the standard response is to rewind to a checkpoint, skip some
					data and try again. Trackers chart the loss; model-risk tools inspect the finished model.
					Nothing in between names the part that broke while it is breaking, so the cause is never
					written down and a validator inherits a black box.
				</p>
				<p class="iv-thesis-p">
					ORMAS watches every component while the model trains. When a part dies, its learning
					signal is exactly zero, and standard training cannot leave that trap. ORMAS names the part,
					resets only that part, and writes the change to a ledger: component, epoch, reason, size.
				</p>
				<p class="iv-thesis-turn">
					Don’t trust us: replay the run yourself at oxiedo.com/black-box. Every figure there is read
					from the archived run files; nothing is simulated in the browser.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band--dark section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="iv-stats">
				{stats.map((s) => (
					<div class="iv-stat">
						<p class="iv-stat-figure tabular">
							{s.figure}{s.unit && <span class="iv-stat-unit">{s.unit}</span>}
						</p>
						<p class="iv-stat-label">{s.label}</p>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>WHY NOW</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="iv-h2" id="iv-timeline-h">The demand event has a date on it.</h2>
		<p class="iv-lede">
			The usual answer to “why has nobody built this” is that the incentive did not exist. Here it
			is arriving on a legislated calendar, and an architecture cannot be retrofitted into a model
			that has already been trained, so it has to exist before the date, not after it.
		</p>

		<figure class="iv-chart" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="iv-timeline-h">
			<svg
				viewBox={`0 0 ${CH.w} ${CH.h}`}
				class="iv-chart-svg"
				role="img"
				aria-label="Regulatory calendar from 2026 to 2031. SR 26-2 effective April 2026. EU AI Act Omnibus in force July 2026. EU AI Act Annex III high-risk obligations apply 2 December 2027. Annex I obligations apply 2 August 2028. European Health Data Space first phase 2029, full scope 2031."
			>
				{years.map((y) => (
					<g>
						<line
							x1={x(`${y}-01-01`)} y1={CH.top - 22}
							x2={x(`${y}-01-01`)} y2={CH.h - 20}
							class="iv-chart-grid"
						/>
						<text x={x(`${y}-01-01`)} y={CH.top - 30} class="iv-chart-year">{y}</text>
					</g>
				))}

				<line
					x1={x(today)} y1={CH.top - 22}
					x2={x(today)} y2={CH.h - 20}
					class="iv-chart-now"
				/>
				<text x={x(today)} y={CH.h - 6} class="iv-chart-nowlabel">TODAY</text>

				{events.map((e, i) => {
					const cy = CH.top + i * CH.rowH;
					return (
						<g>
							<text x={CH.labelW - 16} y={cy - 2} class="iv-chart-label">{e.label}</text>
							<text x={CH.labelW - 16} y={cy + 13} class="iv-chart-who">{e.who}</text>
							<line x1={CH.labelW} y1={cy + 1} x2={x(e.date) - 7} y2={cy + 1} class="iv-chart-lead" />
							<circle
								cx={x(e.date)} cy={cy + 1} r="5.5"
								class={e.past ? 'iv-chart-dot iv-chart-dot--past' : 'iv-chart-dot'}
							/>
						</g>
					);
				})}
			</svg>
			<figcaption class="iv-chart-cap">
				Dates in law as at October 2026. Filled marks have already taken effect.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<p class="iv-note">
			Two of these have already happened. In the UK the PRA’s SS1/23 deliberately covers AI models,
			and in the US the FDA lets AI devices change only under a documented change plan. Banks are not
			ready: large EMEA banks run around 365 models, 67% use AI or machine learning, only 29% say
			their framework fits, and 75% put under 5% of staff on AI validation (Deloitte, 2025).
		</p>
