		<h2 class="iv-h2">We write the kill rule first, and follow it.</h2>
		<p class="iv-lede">
			A single-author result is easy to tune until it flatters. So each test gets its rule before
			the run, and the rule stays on the record when it goes against us.
		</p>
		<div class="iv-wait">
			<p class="iv-wait-lead">
				Three pre-set tests have gone against us. Each one changed what we build.
			</p>
			<p class="iv-wait-body">
				April 2026: the predecessor architecture lost to a plain CNN at all four noise levels of a
				purpose-built suite, and zero corrections fired in 42 runs. We rewrote the system. October
				2026: a pilot testing whether disagreement between parts flags mislabelled samples came back
				negative, so V2 moved to parallel parts, and the next pilot must beat plain loss by 0.03
				AUROC, published either way.
			</p>
			<p class="iv-wait-body">
				Also October 2026: the rule for AISTATS was not to submit if ORMAS finished two or more
				points below a plain reset. It finished 4.8 points below on one seed, so the paper was not
				submitted, and V2 keeps only detect and reset. The product is the detector and the record.
			</p>
